<!--
  Bathysphere Hw1: the D-Bus interface of a hardware provider (SPEC.md section 11.5).

  A Hw1 provider is a separately installed system package (for the ONEXPLAYER 3: oxp3-hw, daemon
  oxp3-hwd). It runs as a dedicated non-root system user on the system bus and writes only the sysfs
  files its own package grants to its group (SPEC 11.1, 11.7). Bathysphere's hostd is its only
  Bathysphere client; Steam reaches the provider's steamos-manager remotes through steamos-manager.

  Bus names (all owned by the provider's one connection; the D-Bus policy lets only the provider's
  system user own them):
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1                        main name; one provider per machine
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.TdpLimit1              claimed only while that remote works
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.GpuPerformanceLevel1   claimed only while that remote works
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.GpuPowerProfile1       claimed only while that remote works
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.BatteryChargeLimit1    claimed only while that remote works

  Objects:
    /io/github/featherwolf/Bathysphere/Hw1                  the Hw1 interfaces below
    /io/github/featherwolf/Bathysphere/Hw1/SteamOSManager   steamos-manager remotes (child node below)

  Common rules (every interface):
  - Units: watts (u) for power limits, MHz for frequencies, percent, mW for samples, and enumerated
    strings. No floats, no paths and no free strings in arguments.
  - Setters are methods, so a multi-field change is atomic and carries its caller for leases. State
    is in properties; every property that can change emits org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties
    PropertiesChanged; constant properties are annotated "const".
  - Authorization: every setter, Provider1.Release, Provider1.TakeControl, every property set on a
    remote and PackagePower1.Sample. The provider reads the caller's UnixUserID with
    org.freedesktop.DBus.GetConnectionCredentials on every call (never cached), refuses uid 0, and
    requires it to equal the User of logind's seat0 ActiveSession with Remote false, Class "user"
    and State "active"; otherwise org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.AccessDenied. PIDs, executable paths
    and cgroups are never used for authorization. All other reads are open.
  - Rate: at most 2 committed writes per second per knob (a burst is coalesced: only the newest
    waiting value is written, and every waiting caller gets the reply of that write) and at most 20
    calls per second per sender (beyond that org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.LimitsExceeded).
  - Bounds: a value outside the published bounds is refused with
    org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs and nothing is written. Input is never clamped.
  - Leases: a setter on a leased knob (boost, epp, cpuLimits, socBalance, gpuRange, fan,
    chargeBehaviour) takes or refreshes that knob's lease for the caller's unique bus name; setting
    the knob to its default ends the caller's lease instead. While another connection holds the
    lease, setters fail with Error.Busy "lease:UNIQUE-NAME". A lease ends on Provider1.Release or
    when the holder's unique name vanishes (NameOwnerChanged); the provider then restores the knob's
    default and emits Changed (released, reverted-disconnect). Steam-owned knobs (tdp, gpu,
    gpuPowerProfile, chargeLimit), which the provider serves through the remotes, have no lease.
  - Every accepted write is read back (tolerances: RAPL 250 mW, GPU 25 MHz, CPU caps 100 MHz, all
    others exact). A mismatch fails with Error.NotApplied; there is no rollback, because every
    accepted value is safe. One journal line is written per write: uid, knob, old value, new value
    and read-back.

  Errors (hostd's broker code in brackets, SPEC 11.5):
    org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs       [EINVAL] out of range, unknown key, knob or mode;
                                                  nothing written
    org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.AccessDenied      [EBUSY "session not active"] the seat0 check failed
    org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.LimitsExceeded    [ERATE] more than 20 calls per second from one sender
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.Error.Busy
                                                  [EBUSY] the message names the co-writer
                                                  (power-profiles-daemon, tuned-ppd, hhd, powerstation),
                                                  the lease holder ("lease:" + unique name) or the
                                                  condition (governor, battery, "cooldown:" + seconds,
                                                  "sleep" between logind's PrepareForSleep true and false)
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.Error.NotApplied
                                                  [EBUSY "not applied"] read-back mismatch; the message
                                                  is KNOB=OBSERVED, the knob id and the value read back
                                                  in its unit (decimal) or its word
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.Error.Locked
                                                  [EBUSY] a RAPL constraint refused the write with
                                                  EACCES; the provider skips it for the rest of the boot
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.Error.NotSupported
                                                  [ENOTSUP] the knob is not available on this device
                                                  now; the message is its Describe reason
    io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.Error.Disabled
                                                  [ENOTSUP] the provider's kill switch
                                                  (/etc/PROVIDER/disabled) exists
-->

<node name="/io/github/featherwolf/Bathysphere/Hw1">

  <!--
      io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.Provider1:
      @short_description: Identity, versioning, knob state, leases and change notices of a Hw1 provider.

      Knob ids are SPEC 11.2's: tdp, gpu, gpuPowerProfile and chargeLimit (served through the
      steamos-manager remotes; owned by Steam), boost, epp, cpuLimits, socBalance, gpuRange, fan and
      chargeBehaviour (leased), and packagePower (read only). Any other id is
      org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs.

      Core types (CpuEnergy1, CpuLimits1): "p" is the performance cores (every core of a CPU with
      one core type), "e" the efficient cores that have an L3 cache, "lpe" the low-power efficient
      cores without one, and "all" (CpuEnergy1 only) every CPU policy.

      Versioning: ApiVersion is 1 for this file. hostd uses a provider only when it knows its
      ApiVersion, ProviderName and ProviderVersion are not revoked (the catalog's revocations.json
      "providers" entries and hostd's shipped table), and hostd's own device table has an entry whose
      id equals DeviceId; otherwise every Hw1 knob is ENOTSUP and health shows the provider as
      unrecognised or revoked. Additions that a client of the same ApiVersion can ignore (a new
      interface, property, Describe key or Changed reason) keep ApiVersion; clients ignore unknown
      Describe keys and treat an unknown Changed reason as "set". An incompatible change to one
      interface ships as the next interface name (for example PowerLimits2) beside the old one; a
      change to authorization, leases or safety rules raises ApiVersion. The bus name and object path
      do not change within Hw1.
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.Provider1">

    <!--
        ApiVersion:

        The version of this contract the provider implements: 1.
    -->
    <property name="ApiVersion" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        ProviderName:

        The provider's package name, matching ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,31}$ (for example "oxp3-hw"). The
        catalog's revocation entries name a provider by it.
    -->
    <property name="ProviderName" type="s" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        ProviderVersion:

        The provider's package version, a semver 2.0 string of at most 64 characters (for example
        "1.0.0").
    -->
    <property name="ProviderVersion" type="s" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        DeviceId:

        The id of the compiled device entry whose DMI match made the provider export anything,
        matching ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$ (for example "onexplayer-3"). A provider exports nothing
        without an exact DMI match, so this is never empty.
    -->
    <property name="DeviceId" type="s" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        Knobs:

        The ids of the knobs that are available now, in the order of SPEC 11.2 (Describe reports
        available = true for exactly these). It changes when a grant, a kernel attribute or a
        co-writer appears or goes; the provider then also emits Changed with "available" or
        "unavailable".
    -->
    <property name="Knobs" type="as" access="read"/>

    <!--
        Describe:
        @knob: a knob id (see the interface description).
        @description: the knob's state; keys are left out where they do not apply.

        Open to every caller. Keys of @description:
        - "available" (b): whether the knob can be used now. Always present.
        - "reason" (s): "" when available, else one of: "kernel" (the kernel does not expose the
          attribute, for example fan and charge before Linux 7.4 on the ONEXPLAYER 3), "grants" (the
          attribute exists but the provider's group cannot write it), "locked" (a RAPL constraint
          refused writes this boot), "device" (this device's entry has no such knob), "disabled"
          (the kill switch exists), or "busy:" + a co-writer (power-profiles-daemon, tuned-ppd,
          hhd, powerstation). Always present.
        - "unit" (s): the unit of min, max and numeric values: "W", "MHz", "percent", "mW",
          "level" (a dimensionless step) or "enum".
        - "min", "max" (u): the bounds of the knob's main number, the intersection of the compiled
          hard limit, the device entry, the kernel or firmware range and the admin envelope.
        - "values" (as): the allowed choices of the knob's enumerated part.
        - "value" (v): the knob's held value. "effective" (v, tdp and boost only): what the
          hardware applies now when a rule makes it differ from value (tdp: PL1 while capped on
          battery; boost: the PL2 computed from the mode). "default" (v, leased knobs only): what
          Release or the end of a lease restores.
        - "lease" (s, leased knobs only): the unique bus name of the lease holder, or "".
        - "overridden" (b): another writer changed the value twice in a row outside the grace
          windows and the provider stopped re-asserting it (cleared by TakeControl or a new set
          from the lease holder). Always present.
        - "source" (s): a short citation of where the bound comes from, compiled with the device
          entry; shown in health, never parsed.
        - "persistence" (s): "volatile" (held until changed; the firmware value after reboot),
          "lease" (held only while the lease holder is connected) or "persisted" (stored and
          re-applied at provider start).

        Types and parts per knob (unit; value and default; min and max; values):
        - tdp: W; u, PL1 in W (value is the target Steam set); TdpLimitMin..TdpLimitMax; none.
        - gpu: MHz; s, the GpuPerformanceLevel1 level; ManualGpuClockMin..ManualGpuClockMax;
          AvailableGpuPerformanceLevels.
        - gpuPowerProfile: enum; s; none; AvailableGpuPowerProfiles.
        - chargeLimit: percent; u; SuggestedMinimumLimit..100; none.
        - boost: W; s, the mode (effective: u, PL2 in W); BoostLimitMin..BoostLimitMax;
          AvailableBoostModes.
        - epp: enum; a{ss} as EnergyPreference; none; AvailableEnergyPreferences.
        - cpuLimits: percent; u, MaxPerfPct; MaxPerfPctMin..100; none.
        - socBalance: level; (uu), balance and offset; BalanceMin..BalanceMax; none.
        - gpuRange: MHz; (uu), AutoMinFreq and AutoMaxFreq; FreqMin..FreqMax; none.
        - fan: enum; s; none; AvailableFanModes.
        - chargeBehaviour: enum; s; none; AvailableChargeBehaviours.
        - packagePower: mW; u, the latest sample; none; none.
    -->
    <method name="Describe">
      <arg name="knob" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="description" type="a{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
        Release:
        @knobs: leased knob ids; an empty array means every knob the caller holds.

        Authorized. For each listed knob the caller holds, drops the caller's lease and, unless the
        knob is marked overridden, writes its default (Describe's "default": the device entry's
        compiled default, or the boot snapshot where the entry has none), then emits
        Changed(knob, "released"). Knobs the caller does not
        hold are skipped. An id that is not a leased knob is
        org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs and nothing is released.
    -->
    <method name="Release">
      <arg name="knobs" type="as" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!--
        TakeControl:
        @knobs: knob ids, at least one.

        Authorized. Clears "overridden" for each knob and writes its held value once (hostd calls it
        only on a user action in Home). A leased knob held by another connection fails with
        Error.Busy "lease:UNIQUE-NAME" and nothing changes.
    -->
    <method name="TakeControl">
      <arg name="knobs" type="as" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!--
        Changed:
        @knob: the knob id.
        @reason: why it changed.

        Emitted after every change of a knob's state, together with PropertiesChanged. Reasons:
        "set" (a setter or a remote property set succeeded), "released" (Release or a default
        set), "reasserted-ac" and "reasserted-resume" (the provider wrote the held value again
        after a power-source change or a resume, inside its grace window), "overridden" (two
        consecutive mismatches outside a grace window; re-asserting stopped), "reverted-temperature"
        (fan back to auto at 90 °C or above), "reverted-sensor" (fan back to auto because the package
        temperature could not be read), "reverted-sleep" (fan back to auto before sleep),
        "reverted-disconnect" (the lease holder's name vanished), "reverted-stop" (the provider is
        stopping), "capped-battery" (an AC-only value was lowered to the battery maximum),
        "raised-with-sustained" (PL2 raised because a new PL1 was above it), "available" and
        "unavailable" (the knob entered or left Knobs).
    -->
    <signal name="Changed">
      <arg name="knob" type="s"/>
      <arg name="reason" type="s"/>
    </signal>

  </interface>

  <!--
      io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.PowerLimits1:
      @short_description: Boost power (RAPL PL2, short_term) relative to Steam's sustained limit.

      Knob "boost" (leased). The provider writes both package zones (intel-rapl-mmio:0 before
      intel-rapl:0), PL2 before PL1 when raising and PL1 before PL2 when lowering, and keeps
      PL2 at or above PL1. It never writes PL4, time windows, "enabled" or any other zone. Steam's
      sustained limit (PL1) is the TdpLimit1 remote's TdpLimit.
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.PowerLimits1">

    <!--
        AvailableBoostModes:

        The modes this device supports, in this order, from "firmware", "follow" and "manual".
    -->
    <property name="AvailableBoostModes" type="as" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        BoostMode:

        The current mode: "firmware" (PL2 is the device entry's compiled default, 52 W on the
        ONEXPLAYER 3, never read from registers; this is the default and ends the lease), "follow"
        (PL2 = min(PL1 + BoostOffset, BoostLimitMax), recomputed on every PL1 change, Steam's
        through the remote included) or "manual" (PL2 = max(BoostLimit, PL1)).
    -->
    <property name="BoostMode" type="s" access="read"/>

    <!--
        BoostLimit:

        The PL2 limit in W used by "manual"; in other modes the last manual value, initially the
        compiled default.
    -->
    <property name="BoostLimit" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
        BoostOffset:

        The offset in W above PL1 used by "follow"; initially 0.
    -->
    <property name="BoostOffset" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
        EffectiveBoostLimit:

        The PL2 in W the provider holds now.
    -->
    <property name="EffectiveBoostLimit" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
        BoostLimitMin:

        The smallest manual limit in W (5 on the ONEXPLAYER 3).
    -->
    <property name="BoostLimitMin" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        BoostLimitMax:

        The largest PL2 in W in any mode (52 on the ONEXPLAYER 3).
    -->
    <property name="BoostLimitMax" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        BoostOffsetMax:

        The largest "follow" offset in W, from the device entry.
    -->
    <property name="BoostOffsetMax" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        SetBoost:
        @mode: one of AvailableBoostModes.
        @limit_w: with "manual", the limit in [BoostLimitMin, BoostLimitMax]; otherwise 0.
        @offset_w: with "follow", the offset in [0, BoostOffsetMax]; otherwise 0.
        @effective_w: the PL2 in W written and read back.

        Authorized; takes the "boost" lease ("firmware" ends it). A non-zero argument that the mode
        does not use, an unknown mode or a value out of range is
        org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs. Errors also: Busy (lease holder, hhd, powerstation),
        NotApplied, Locked, NotSupported, Disabled.
    -->
    <method name="SetBoost">
      <arg name="mode" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="limit_w" type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="offset_w" type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="effective_w" type="u" direction="out"/>
    </method>

  </interface>

  <!--
      io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.CpuEnergy1:
      @short_description: CPU energy-performance preference (EPP) per core type.

      Knob "epp" (leased). Unavailable (reason "busy:power-profiles-daemon" or "busy:tuned-ppd")
      while org.freedesktop.UPower.PowerProfiles has an owner, because that daemon writes EPP
      itself; hostd then offers coarse EPP through its holds instead. The default is the boot
      snapshot.
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.CpuEnergy1">

    <!--
        CoreTypes:

        "all" followed by the core types this CPU has, in the order "p", "e", "lpe".
    -->
    <property name="CoreTypes" type="as" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        AvailableEnergyPreferences:

        The kernel's energy_performance_available_preferences list, in its order.
    -->
    <property name="AvailableEnergyPreferences" type="as" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        EnergyPreference:

        For each core type except "all", the preference its first policy reads back. A value the
        kernel shows as a raw number (0 to 255) is reported as that decimal string.
    -->
    <property name="EnergyPreference" type="a{ss}" access="read"/>

    <!--
        SetEnergyPreference:
        @preferences: either "all" alone or any of the per-type keys of CoreTypes, each mapped to a
        member of AvailableEnergyPreferences.

        Authorized; takes the "epp" lease. An empty map, an unknown key, "all" together with a
        per-type key, or an unknown value is org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs. Errors also:
        Busy "power-profiles-daemon" or "tuned-ppd" (the PowerProfiles name has an owner), Busy
        "governor" (the CPU governor is "performance", so EPP cannot change), Busy (lease holder),
        NotApplied, NotSupported, Disabled.
    -->
    <method name="SetEnergyPreference">
      <arg name="preferences" type="a{ss}" direction="in"/>
    </method>

  </interface>

  <!--
      io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.CpuLimits1:
      @short_description: CPU performance caps and floor (intel_pstate max_perf_pct and
      min_perf_pct) and per-core-type maximum frequency (scaling_max_freq).

      Knob "cpuLimits" (leased). The default is the boot snapshot. The provider never writes
      scaling_min_freq, no_turbo (steamos-manager's CpuBoost1 owns it) or intel_pstate/status.
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.CpuLimits1">

    <!--
        MaxPerfPct:

        intel_pstate max_perf_pct as read back, in percent.
    -->
    <property name="MaxPerfPct" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
        MinPerfPct:

        intel_pstate min_perf_pct as read back, in percent.
    -->
    <property name="MinPerfPct" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
        MaxPerfPctMin:

        The smallest allowed MaxPerfPct: the hard limit, 30.
    -->
    <property name="MaxPerfPctMin" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        CoreTypeMaxFreq:

        For each core type except "all", scaling_max_freq of its first policy in MHz.
    -->
    <property name="CoreTypeMaxFreq" type="a{su}" access="read"/>

    <!--
        CoreTypeFreqRange:

        For each core type except "all", (cpuinfo_min_freq, cpuinfo_max_freq) of its policies in
        MHz.
    -->
    <property name="CoreTypeFreqRange" type="a{s(uu)}" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        SetCpuLimits:
        @limits: at least one of "max_perf_pct" (u), "min_perf_pct" (u) and "core_type_max_freq"
        (a{su}, MHz per core type).

        Authorized; takes the "cpuLimits" lease. Rules:
        - max_perf_pct in [MaxPerfPctMin, 100].
        - min_perf_pct at most the device entry's AC floor cap (50 on the ONEXPLAYER 3) and at
          most the resulting max_perf_pct. A value above the boot snapshot is accepted only on AC
          (else Busy "battery") and is restored to the snapshot on unplug (Changed
          "capped-battery").
        - core_type_max_freq keys are CoreTypes other than "all", values inside CoreTypeFreqRange.
          While intel_pstate/no_turbo reads 1 the kernel limits each policy to its base_frequency,
          so the read-back is compared with min(value, base_frequency).
        - A rising cap is written before the floor, otherwise the floor first.
        An unknown key, a wrong variant type or a value out of range is
        org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs. Errors also: Busy (lease holder, hhd, powerstation),
        NotApplied, NotSupported, Disabled.
    -->
    <method name="SetCpuLimits">
      <arg name="limits" type="a{sv}" direction="in"/>
    </method>

  </interface>

  <!--
      io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.SocBalance1:
      @short_description: The SoC power slider's balance and offset (processor_thermal_soc_slider).

      Knob "socBalance" (leased). The values reach the firmware only through a write of "balanced"
      to the platform-profile handler named "SoC Power Slider", and power-profiles-daemon owns the
      choice of profile, so the provider writes the profile word only when it already reads
      "balanced". It never writes another word or the legacy /sys/firmware/acpi/platform_profile.
      The default is the boot snapshot.
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.SocBalance1">

    <!--
        Balance:

        The held slider_balance value.
    -->
    <property name="Balance" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
        Offset:

        The held slider_offset value.
    -->
    <property name="Offset" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
        BalanceMin:

        The smallest balance (1 on the ONEXPLAYER 3; the kernel refuses 0).
    -->
    <property name="BalanceMin" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        BalanceMax:

        The largest balance (5 on the ONEXPLAYER 3; the kernel refuses 6).
    -->
    <property name="BalanceMax" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        OffsetMax:

        The largest offset (3 on the ONEXPLAYER 3); the smallest is 0.
    -->
    <property name="OffsetMax" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        Active:

        True while the "SoC Power Slider" profile reads "balanced", so the held values are in
        effect.
    -->
    <property name="Active" type="b" access="read"/>

    <!--
        SetSocBalance:
        @balance: in [BalanceMin, BalanceMax].
        @offset: in [0, OffsetMax].

        Authorized; takes the "socBalance" lease. Writes slider_offset, then slider_balance (both as
        two hex digits), then "balanced" into the "SoC Power Slider" profile only if it reads
        "balanced" now; otherwise the parameters are held (and written), Active is false, and they
        take effect at the next "balanced". Errors: InvalidArgs, Busy (lease holder),
        NotApplied, NotSupported, Disabled.
    -->
    <method name="SetSocBalance">
      <arg name="balance" type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="offset" type="u" direction="in"/>
    </method>

  </interface>

  <!--
      io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.GpuRange1:
      @short_description: The GPU frequency range used while Steam's GPU level is "auto".

      Knob "gpuRange" (leased). Applies to the render GT only (never the media GT). It takes
      effect only while the provider's GpuPerformanceLevel1 remote is at "auto"; Steam's "manual"
      pins min = max and wins, and returning to "auto" writes this range again. The default is the
      boot range (rpn..rp0).
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.GpuRange1">

    <!--
        AutoMinFreq:

        The lower end of the "auto" range in MHz.
    -->
    <property name="AutoMinFreq" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
        AutoMaxFreq:

        The upper end of the "auto" range in MHz.
    -->
    <property name="AutoMaxFreq" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
        FreqMin:

        The render GT's rpn in MHz, read at provider start.
    -->
    <property name="FreqMin" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        FreqMax:

        The render GT's rp0 in MHz, read at provider start.
    -->
    <property name="FreqMax" type="u" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        SetAutoRange:
        @min_mhz: in [FreqMin, max_mhz].
        @max_mhz: in [min_mhz, FreqMax].

        Authorized; takes the "gpuRange" lease. While the level is "auto" it writes max_freq first
        when raising and min_freq first when lowering, so min stays at or below max; otherwise it
        holds the range. The kernel's EAGAIN (GPU resetting or resuming) is retried 3 times 0.3 s
        apart, then NotApplied. Errors: InvalidArgs, Busy (lease holder, hhd, powerstation),
        NotApplied, NotSupported, Disabled.
    -->
    <method name="SetAutoRange">
      <arg name="min_mhz" type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="max_mhz" type="u" direction="in"/>
    </method>

  </interface>

  <!--
      io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.FanMode1:
      @short_description: Fan mode, with safety reverts that do not depend on any client.

      Knob "fan" (leased; the default is "auto"). On the ONEXPLAYER 3 (oxpec hwmon "oxp_ec",
      Linux 7.4 or later): "auto" writes pwm1_enable 2, "quiet" writes pwm1_enable 1 and then pwm1
      = the device's quiet duty (102; the hard limit is 80 to 255), "max" writes pwm1_enable 0
      (full speed). While the fan is not "auto" the provider holds the logind delay inhibitor
      Inhibit("sleep", PROVIDER, "fan back to auto", "delay"). It returns the fan to "auto", emits
      Changed and sets LastRevert when: the package temperature (coretemp "Package id 0", checked
      every 1 s) is 90 °C or more ("temperature") or cannot be read ("sensor"); logind sends
      PrepareForSleep(true) ("sleep", then the inhibitor is released); the lease holder's name
      vanishes ("disconnect"); the provider gets SIGTERM ("stop"). A hang ends in a watchdog kill
      (only the safety task feeds the watchdog) and a crash in ExecStopPost, which both set "auto".
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.FanMode1">

    <!--
        AvailableFanModes:

        The modes this device supports, in this order, from "auto", "quiet" and "max".
    -->
    <property name="AvailableFanModes" type="as" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        FanMode:

        The current mode, as read back.
    -->
    <property name="FanMode" type="s" access="read"/>

    <!--
        LastRevert:

        Why the provider last returned the fan to "auto": "temperature", "sensor", "sleep",
        "disconnect" or "stop"; "" when it has not since the last successful SetFanMode with a
        mode other than "auto".
    -->
    <property name="LastRevert" type="s" access="read"/>

    <!--
        SetFanMode:
        @mode: one of AvailableFanModes.

        Authorized. A mode other than "auto" takes the "fan" lease on the caller's unique name;
        "auto" ends it. "quiet" is refused for 600 s after a "temperature" revert (Busy
        "cooldown:" + the seconds left). Between logind's PrepareForSleep(true) and (false) only
        "auto" is accepted (Busy "sleep"). Errors: InvalidArgs, Busy (lease holder, cooldown, sleep),
        NotApplied, NotSupported, Disabled.
    -->
    <method name="SetFanMode">
      <arg name="mode" type="s" direction="in"/>
    </method>

  </interface>

  <!--
      io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.BatteryChargeBehaviour1:
      @short_description: Pause charging while the device is awake ("run from the charger").

      Knob "chargeBehaviour" (leased; the default is "auto"). Writes the battery's
      charge_behaviour (Linux 7.4 or later on the ONEXPLAYER 3). The provider has no code path for
      "inhibit-charge" or "force-discharge". A non-auto value returns to "auto" when the lease
      holder's name vanishes ("reverted-disconnect"), on SIGTERM ("reverted-stop"), and through
      ExecStopPost after a crash or a watchdog kill.
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.BatteryChargeBehaviour1">

    <!--
        AvailableChargeBehaviours:

        The values this device supports, in this order, from "auto" and "inhibit-charge-awake".
    -->
    <property name="AvailableChargeBehaviours" type="as" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
    </property>

    <!--
        ChargeBehaviour:

        The current value, as read back.
    -->
    <property name="ChargeBehaviour" type="s" access="read"/>

    <!--
        SetChargeBehaviour:
        @behaviour: one of AvailableChargeBehaviours.

        Authorized. "inhibit-charge-awake" takes the "chargeBehaviour" lease on the caller's unique
        name; "auto" ends it. Errors: InvalidArgs, Busy (lease holder), NotApplied, NotSupported,
        Disabled.
    -->
    <method name="SetChargeBehaviour">
      <arg name="behaviour" type="s" direction="in"/>
    </method>

  </interface>

  <!--
      io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.PackagePower1:
      @short_description: CPU package power, coarse enough not to leak secrets.

      Knob "packagePower" (read only). The provider derives it from the package zone's energy_uj,
      which its group may read and others may not (CVE-2020-8694).
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.PackagePower1">

    <!--
        Sample:
        @milliwatts: the average package power over the window, rounded to 100 mW.
        @window_ms: the window in milliseconds, at least 1000.

        Authorized. At most one fresh sample per second across all callers; a call within the
        same second gets the latest sample again. Counter wrap-around is handled with the zone's
        max_energy_range_uj. Errors: AccessDenied, LimitsExceeded, NotSupported, Disabled.
    -->
    <method name="Sample">
      <arg name="milliwatts" type="u" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="window_ms" type="u" direction="out"/>
    </method>

  </interface>

  <!--
      Child object /io/github/featherwolf/Bathysphere/Hw1/SteamOSManager. (This comment names no
      symbol on purpose: gdbus-codegen skips nested nodes and fails on a doc comment naming one.)

      The steamos-manager remotes the provider serves to fill gaps (SPEC 11.6). steamos-manager's
      user daemon finds them through the provider's remotes.d file (one section per interface, each
      with its own bus name and this object path), pings org.freedesktop.DBus.Peer at "/", loads a
      remote when its bus name gets an owner and drops it when the name goes; a remote never
      overrides a local implementation. The interfaces and their member types are Valve's
      (com.steampowered.SteamOSManager1.xml in steamos-manager, MIT; identical in 26.4.1 and
      master); the comments state the provider's rules. Property sets are authorized like Hw1
      setters (the user daemon makes them as the session uid) and read back.
  -->
  <node name="SteamOSManager">

    <!--
        com.steampowered.SteamOSManager1.TdpLimit1:
        @short_description: Steam's sustained power limit (RAPL PL1, long_term).

        Knob "tdp" (Steam-owned). Bus name io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.TdpLimit1, claimed
        only while both package zones (intel-rapl-mmio:0 and intel-rapl:0) have writable long_term
        and short_term constraints. The provider holds the target and re-asserts it after AC
        changes (2 s debounce, 20 s grace) and resume (+3 s, verified at +10 s); outside those
        windows two consecutive mismatches mark it overridden. A lower value from thermald is never
        fought.
    -->
    <interface name="com.steampowered.SteamOSManager1.TdpLimit1">

      <!--
          TdpLimit:

          The sustained limit in W, in [TdpLimitMin, TdpLimitMax] (else InvalidArgs). Writing the
          value it already holds only re-checks both zones. A value above the effective PL2 raises
          PL2 first (Changed "raised-with-sustained"). With an admin AC limit above the battery
          maximum, the provider holds the target and applies the battery maximum while on battery
          (Changed "capped-battery").
      -->
      <property name="TdpLimit" type="u" access="readwrite"/>

      <!--
          TdpLimitMin:

          The smallest sustained limit in W (5 on the ONEXPLAYER 3). Constant.
      -->
      <property name="TdpLimitMin" type="u" access="read">
        <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
      </property>

      <!--
          TdpLimitMax:

          The largest sustained limit in W: the battery envelope (35 on the ONEXPLAYER 3), or the
          admin AC limit when /etc/PROVIDER/limits.toml sets one. Constant, because steamos-manager
          treats it as constant.
      -->
      <property name="TdpLimitMax" type="u" access="read">
        <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
      </property>

    </interface>

    <!--
        com.steampowered.SteamOSManager1.GpuPerformanceLevel1:
        @short_description: Steam's GPU level and manual clock.

        Knob "gpu" (Steam-owned). Bus name
        io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.GpuPerformanceLevel1, claimed only while the render
        GT's min_freq and max_freq are writable. Set GpuPerformanceLevel before ManualGpuClock.
    -->
    <interface name="com.steampowered.SteamOSManager1.GpuPerformanceLevel1">

      <!--
          AvailableGpuPerformanceLevels:

          ["auto", "manual"].
      -->
      <property name="AvailableGpuPerformanceLevels" type="as" access="read">
        <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
      </property>

      <!--
          GpuPerformanceLevel:

          "auto" writes the GpuRange1 range (by default the boot range rpn..rp0); "manual" pins
          min_freq = max_freq = ManualGpuClock.
      -->
      <property name="GpuPerformanceLevel" type="s" access="readwrite"/>

      <!--
          ManualGpuClock:

          The pinned clock in MHz used by "manual", in [ManualGpuClockMin, ManualGpuClockMax].
      -->
      <property name="ManualGpuClock" type="u" access="readwrite"/>

      <!--
          ManualGpuClockMin:

          rpn of the render GT in MHz.
      -->
      <property name="ManualGpuClockMin" type="u" access="read">
        <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
      </property>

      <!--
          ManualGpuClockMax:

          rp0 of the render GT in MHz.
      -->
      <property name="ManualGpuClockMax" type="u" access="read">
        <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
      </property>

    </interface>

    <!--
        com.steampowered.SteamOSManager1.GpuPowerProfile1:
        @short_description: The xe GPU power profile.

        Knob "gpuPowerProfile" (Steam-owned). Bus name
        io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.GpuPowerProfile1, claimed only while the render GT's
        freq0/power_profile is writable.
    -->
    <interface name="com.steampowered.SteamOSManager1.GpuPowerProfile1">

      <!--
          AvailableGpuPowerProfiles:

          ["base", "power_saving"].
      -->
      <property name="AvailableGpuPowerProfiles" type="as" access="read">
        <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
      </property>

      <!--
          GpuPowerProfile:

          One of AvailableGpuPowerProfiles; the kernel's EAGAIN is retried like GpuRange1.
      -->
      <property name="GpuPowerProfile" type="s" access="readwrite"/>

    </interface>

    <!--
        com.steampowered.SteamOSManager1.BatteryChargeLimit1:
        @short_description: Steam's battery charge limit.

        Knob "chargeLimit" (Steam-owned). Bus name
        io.github.featherwolf.Bathysphere.Hw1.BatteryChargeLimit1, claimed only while the
        battery's charge_control_end_threshold is writable (Linux 7.4 or later on the ONEXPLAYER 3).
        The only value the provider persists: a threshold at or above SuggestedMinimumLimit is
        stored and re-applied at provider start.
    -->
    <interface name="com.steampowered.SteamOSManager1.BatteryChargeLimit1">

      <!--
          MaxChargeLevel:

          The end-of-charge threshold in percent. -1 means "no limit" and writes 100 (never 0,
          which blocks charging on this hardware); any other value must lie in
          [SuggestedMinimumLimit, 100]. Reads report the threshold read back.
      -->
      <property name="MaxChargeLevel" type="i" access="readwrite"/>

      <!--
          SuggestedMinimumLimit:

          The device entry's smallest threshold (50 on the ONEXPLAYER 3).
      -->
      <property name="SuggestedMinimumLimit" type="i" access="read">
        <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="const"/>
      </property>

    </interface>

  </node>

</node>
